### Ember.js Code Snippet (`config/environment.js`)

This is the most critical part of your Ember.js application code that directly influences deployment, specifically how URLs are handled when served by a web server like Nginx.

```javascript
// config/environment.js

module.exports = function(environment) {
  let ENV = {
    modulePrefix: 'aiby-os',
    environment,
    // --- Deployment-related configurations ---
    rootURL: '/', // Determines the base URL for all assets and routing.
                  // '/' means the app expects to be served from the root of the domain (e.g., https://aiby.bearsmithnetwork.com/)
                  // If your app was in a subfolder like /app/, it would be '/app/'.
    locationType: 'history', // Uses HTML5 History API for clean URLs (e.g., /dashboard instead of /#/dashboard).
                            // Requires server-side configuration (like Nginx's try_files) to redirect unknown paths to index.html.
    // ---------------------------------------
    EmberENV: {
      FEATURES: {
        // Here you can enable/disable Ember features
      },
      EXTEND_PROTOTYPES: {
        // Prevent Ember Data from overriding Date.parse.
        Date: false
      }
    },

    APP: {
      // Here you can pass flags/options to your application instance
      // when it is created
    }
  };

  if (environment === 'development') {
    // ENV.APP.LOG_RESOLVER = true;
    // ENV.APP.LOG_ACTIVE_GENERATION = true;
    // ENV.APP.LOG_TRANSITIONS = true;
    // ENV.APP.LOG_TRANSITIONS_INTERNAL = true;
    // ENV.APP.LOG_VIEW_LOOKUPS = true;
  }

  if (environment === 'test') {
    // Testem prefers this setting.
    ENV.locationType = 'none';

    // keep test console output quieter
    ENV.APP.LOG_ACTIVE_GENERATION = false;
    ENV.APP.LOG_VIEW_LOOKUPS = false;

    ENV.APP.rootElement = '#ember-testing';
    ENV.APP.autoboot = false;
  }

  if (environment === 'production') {
    // Add production-specific configurations here
    // For example, API endpoints, analytics IDs, etc.
    // ENV.apiHost = 'https://api.aiby.bearsmithnetwork.com';
    // ENV.someSecretKey = process.env.AIBY_OS_SECRET_KEY; // Using environment variables for secrets
  }

  return ENV;
};
```

**Explanation of the snippet:**

*   **`rootURL: '/'`**: This tells Ember where the application expects to be served from. If your AIBY OS app is accessed directly at `aiby.bearsmithnetwork.com`, then `rootURL: '/'` is correct. If it were `aiby.bearsmithnetwork.com/os/`, then `rootURL: '/os/'` would be needed. This value also dictates the `base` tag in your `index.html`.
*   **`locationType: 'history'`**: This utilizes the HTML5 History API to provide clean, hash-less URLs (e.g., `/dashboard` instead of `/#/dashboard`). For this to work, your web server (Nginx) must be configured to fall back to `index.html` for any paths that don't directly map to a static file. This is handled by the `try_files $uri $uri/ /index.html =404;` directive in the Nginx config.

By following these steps, your AIBY OS Ember app will be successfully deployed, served securely via HTTPS, and accessible to users on the `bearsmithnetwork` server.
